1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
In the early 1970s,
every time you boarded a passenger plane,

2
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:06,060
your chance of dying on that flight

3
00:00:06,290 --> 00:00:09,865
was about one in three hundred
and fifty thousand.

4
00:00:10,165 --> 00:00:12,905
Today, it's about one in fourteen million.

5
00:00:13,611 --> 00:00:17,991
In a single lifetime, flying has
become roughly forty times safer.

6
00:00:18,349 --> 00:00:20,429
And it's not because planes
stopped breaking.

7
00:00:20,509 --> 00:00:22,449
Engines still fail.

8
00:00:22,529 --> 00:00:24,369
Sensors still freeze.

9
00:00:24,749 --> 00:00:26,469
Pilots still make mistakes.

10
00:00:27,346 --> 00:00:29,026
So what actually changed?

11
00:00:30,506 --> 00:00:34,406
Almost every rule on a modern flight
exists for the same reason.

12
00:00:35,039 --> 00:00:36,879
Somebody, somewhere, died.

13
00:00:38,274 --> 00:00:40,514
But that alone doesn't explain it.

14
00:00:41,114 --> 00:00:45,954
People die on roads too, and roads haven't
become anything like forty times safer.

15
00:00:46,407 --> 00:00:50,067
So the real question isn't
whether aviation learns from its crashes.

16
00:00:50,887 --> 00:00:53,267
It's how it manages to learn so well.

17
00:00:55,697 --> 00:01:00,577
On the thirtieth of October 1935,
Boeing showed the US Army its new bomber.

18
00:01:00,807 --> 00:01:03,577
The Model 299.

19
00:01:04,619 --> 00:01:07,199
Four engines, retractable landing gear,

20
00:01:07,279 --> 00:01:11,159
and far more switches and settings
than any pilot was used to.

21
00:01:11,627 --> 00:01:15,247
Seconds after takeoff,
it climbed steeply, stalled, and crashed.

22
00:01:15,587 --> 00:01:18,127
Two of the five men on board died.

23
00:01:18,786 --> 00:01:20,466
The pilots weren't careless.

24
00:01:21,186 --> 00:01:22,986
They had simply forgotten
to release a lock

25
00:01:23,216 --> 00:01:25,706
that held the controls in place
on the ground.

26
00:01:26,436 --> 00:01:30,056
A newspaper called it "too much
airplane for one man to fly".

27
00:01:30,756 --> 00:01:32,556
The answer wasn't more training.

28
00:01:33,056 --> 00:01:34,256
It was a card.

29
00:01:34,496 --> 00:01:38,496
A short list of things to check
before takeoff, and before landing.

30
00:01:39,112 --> 00:01:41,172
The Army bought the bomber anyway.

31
00:01:41,732 --> 00:01:45,432
It became the B-17,
and its crews flew it with checklists.

32
00:01:46,186 --> 00:01:48,346
Today,
a captain with twenty thousand hours

33
00:01:48,576 --> 00:01:52,106
still reads a list
to make sure the landing gear is down.

34
00:01:52,703 --> 00:01:54,943
Not because they don't know how to fly.

35
00:01:55,143 --> 00:01:59,803
But because knowing what to do isn't
the same as doing it, every single time.

36
00:02:02,253 --> 00:02:05,033
In 1952, Britain's de Havilland Comet

37
00:02:05,263 --> 00:02:08,653
became the first jet airliner
to carry paying passengers.

38
00:02:09,693 --> 00:02:13,013
It flew higher and faster
than any airliner before it,

39
00:02:13,093 --> 00:02:14,533
above most of the weather.

40
00:02:15,151 --> 00:02:18,491
Then, in early 1954,
two Comets broke apart in mid-air,

41
00:02:18,571 --> 00:02:20,411
within three months of each other.

42
00:02:21,191 --> 00:02:24,871
No storm. No collision. No warning.

43
00:02:25,492 --> 00:02:27,292
Every Comet was grounded.

44
00:02:28,052 --> 00:02:31,232
Investigators put a complete one
into a giant tank of water,

45
00:02:31,462 --> 00:02:35,412
and pressurised the cabin
and released it, over and over.

46
00:02:35,979 --> 00:02:41,023
After the equivalent of about three
thousand flights, the fuselage tore open.

47
00:02:41,323 --> 00:02:44,703
The crack had started at the corner
of an opening in the fuselage.

48
00:02:45,103 --> 00:02:49,263
Every flight stretched the metal there,
a little, until it gave way.

49
00:02:49,883 --> 00:02:51,083
Metal fatigue.

50
00:02:52,013 --> 00:02:56,313
From then on, every new airliner
had to be designed against metal fatigue,

51
00:02:56,633 --> 00:02:59,153
and its makers had to show
that it would hold.

52
00:03:00,474 --> 00:03:04,354
There was a second lesson in the Comets,
and it came from a young Australian

53
00:03:04,584 --> 00:03:08,074
scientist who'd been asked to help
work out what had gone wrong.

54
00:03:08,677 --> 00:03:12,297
David Warren noticed
that the investigators could only guess.

55
00:03:12,837 --> 00:03:16,577
Nobody knew what the crew had seen or said
in their last moments.

56
00:03:17,044 --> 00:03:18,584
So he built a device that recorded

57
00:03:18,814 --> 00:03:21,324
the instruments
and the voices in the cockpit,

58
00:03:21,444 --> 00:03:23,044
and would survive the crash.

59
00:03:23,753 --> 00:03:25,653
We still call it the black box.

60
00:03:26,193 --> 00:03:29,593
It's actually bright orange,
so it can be found in the wreckage.

61
00:03:30,115 --> 00:03:31,735
And once investigators could hear

62
00:03:31,965 --> 00:03:34,735
what went on inside a cockpit,
they found problems

63
00:03:34,965 --> 00:03:36,855
that weren't in the machine at all.

64
00:03:39,445 --> 00:03:42,265
On the twenty-seventh of March 1977,

65
00:03:42,345 --> 00:03:47,965
two Boeing 747s were on the same runway
at Tenerife, in the Canary Islands.

66
00:03:48,645 --> 00:03:52,285
The fog was so thick
that neither crew could see the other.

67
00:03:52,813 --> 00:03:56,240
The KLM jet began its takeoff roll.

68
00:03:56,393 --> 00:03:59,973
The Pan Am jet was still on the runway,
taxiing towards it.

69
00:04:00,505 --> 00:04:03,905
The KLM captain believed
he'd been cleared to take off.

70
00:04:04,505 --> 00:04:05,705
He hadn't.

71
00:04:06,107 --> 00:04:10,087
The tower had said "OK",
and then "stand by for takeoff".

72
00:04:10,447 --> 00:04:15,387
But the second half was lost, because
another radio call came in on top of it.

73
00:04:15,955 --> 00:04:20,375
In the cockpit, the flight engineer asked,
"Is he not clear, that Pan American?"

74
00:04:20,795 --> 00:04:23,875
The captain answered, "Oh, yes."
And kept going.

75
00:04:24,615 --> 00:04:27,315
Five hundred and eighty-three people died.

76
00:04:27,935 --> 00:04:31,095
It's still the deadliest accident
in the history of aviation.

77
00:04:33,685 --> 00:04:37,485
Two things changed.
The first was a single word.

78
00:04:38,157 --> 00:04:41,137
Today, pilots and controllers
only say "takeoff"

79
00:04:41,217 --> 00:04:44,057
when an aircraft is actually cleared
to take off.

80
00:04:44,777 --> 00:04:46,597
Before that, it's a "departure".

81
00:04:47,449 --> 00:04:49,449
The second change took longer.

82
00:04:50,069 --> 00:04:53,769
The KLM captain was the head
of the airline's training for the 747.

83
00:04:54,829 --> 00:04:58,629
His engineer had doubts,
and voiced them. Once.

84
00:04:59,432 --> 00:05:03,072
Out of Tenerife, other crashes like it,
and years of research at NASA,

85
00:05:03,302 --> 00:05:07,072
came a new kind of training,
known as Crew Resource Management.

86
00:05:07,869 --> 00:05:09,649
The captain is still in charge.

87
00:05:09,729 --> 00:05:12,869
But a first officer saying,
"I think that's wrong,"

88
00:05:12,949 --> 00:05:13,929
isn't challenging authority.

89
00:05:14,159 --> 00:05:16,949
They're doing their job.

90
00:05:17,327 --> 00:05:20,367
And sometimes, that teamwork
is all a crew has left.

91
00:05:22,737 --> 00:05:29,137
On the nineteenth of July 1989,
United Flight 232, a DC-10,

92
00:05:29,217 --> 00:05:32,777
was cruising over Iowa
when its tail engine blew apart.

93
00:05:33,393 --> 00:05:37,733
Fragments tore through the tail
and drained all three hydraulic systems.

94
00:05:38,453 --> 00:05:41,713
The pilots could no longer move
a single control surface.

95
00:05:42,365 --> 00:05:45,485
Three separate systems
were meant to make that impossible.

96
00:05:45,845 --> 00:05:49,465
But all three ran through
the same small part of the tail.

97
00:05:50,322 --> 00:05:52,502
The crew found one way left to steer.

98
00:05:53,242 --> 00:05:56,082
More power on one wing engine,
less on the other.

99
00:05:56,628 --> 00:05:59,968
A DC-10 instructor,
travelling as a passenger,

100
00:06:00,048 --> 00:06:03,088
came up to the cockpit
and worked the throttles for them.

101
00:06:03,593 --> 00:06:05,853
They reached the airport at Sioux City.

102
00:06:06,373 --> 00:06:08,073
The landing was a disaster.

103
00:06:08,793 --> 00:06:10,813
The plane cartwheeled and broke apart.

104
00:06:11,829 --> 00:06:13,889
And yet a hundred and eighty-four
of the two

105
00:06:14,119 --> 00:06:17,009
hundred and ninety-six
people on board survived.

106
00:06:17,728 --> 00:06:20,168
Captain Al Haynes later said
a big part of the reason

107
00:06:20,398 --> 00:06:22,748
was the way the four
of them worked together.

108
00:06:23,548 --> 00:06:26,388
The training that came out of crashes
like Tenerife.

109
00:06:28,528 --> 00:06:32,268
By then, computers could fly a plane
more smoothly than any pilot.

110
00:06:32,828 --> 00:06:34,928
That made flying safer.

111
00:06:35,528 --> 00:06:37,548
But it created a new kind of problem.

112
00:06:38,471 --> 00:06:43,371
On the first of June 2009,
Air France Flight 447 was flying

113
00:06:43,601 --> 00:06:48,051
from Rio de Janeiro to Paris,
at night, over the Atlantic.

114
00:06:48,892 --> 00:06:51,952
Ice crystals blocked the sensors
that measure airspeed.

115
00:06:52,792 --> 00:06:55,372
The autopilot switched off,
and suddenly the pilots

116
00:06:55,602 --> 00:06:58,572
were flying by hand,
in the dark, in turbulence.

117
00:06:59,337 --> 00:07:01,917
The pilot at the controls
pulled the nose up.

118
00:07:02,457 --> 00:07:04,677
The plane climbed, slowed, and stalled.

119
00:07:05,408 --> 00:07:07,808
The stall warning sounded again and again.

120
00:07:08,408 --> 00:07:11,048
For more than three minutes,
the plane fell towards the sea

121
00:07:11,278 --> 00:07:13,268
with its nose still pointing up.

122
00:07:13,847 --> 00:07:16,387
Two hundred and twenty-eight people died.

123
00:07:17,007 --> 00:07:19,627
The recorders were found
almost two years later,

124
00:07:19,807 --> 00:07:21,987
almost four kilometres under the sea.

125
00:07:23,028 --> 00:07:24,728
The lesson was uncomfortable.

126
00:07:25,368 --> 00:07:27,568
Automation makes flying safer...

127
00:07:27,728 --> 00:07:31,668
but pilots still have to understand what
it's doing, and be ready when it stops.

128
00:07:34,278 --> 00:07:37,578
So far, every lesson in this story
has cost lives.

129
00:07:38,299 --> 00:07:42,619
But the biggest reason flying is so safe
is that most of its lessons don't.

130
00:07:45,559 --> 00:07:50,639
On the first of December 1974,
a TWA jet descended too early

131
00:07:50,869 --> 00:07:53,579
on its approach to Washington Dulles
and flew into a ridge.

132
00:07:53,809 --> 00:07:56,979
Ninety-two people died.

133
00:07:57,625 --> 00:08:02,665
Six weeks earlier, a United crew had made
the same mistake, on the same approach.

134
00:08:03,005 --> 00:08:06,085
They caught it just in time,
and reported it.

135
00:08:06,419 --> 00:08:09,039
But only United's own pilots were told.

136
00:08:09,679 --> 00:08:12,139
The warning never reached TWA.

137
00:08:12,661 --> 00:08:19,481
So in 1976, America set up a reporting
system run by NASA, not by the regulator.

138
00:08:20,459 --> 00:08:24,119
Pilots, controllers and mechanics
could report their own mistakes,

139
00:08:24,539 --> 00:08:28,019
and in most cases, the report
couldn't be used to punish them.

140
00:08:28,512 --> 00:08:31,332
It has received more
than two million reports.

141
00:08:31,812 --> 00:08:35,852
Close calls, confusing charts,
a checklist that was easy to misread...

142
00:08:36,212 --> 00:08:39,412
each one a lesson,
learned without anyone getting hurt.

143
00:08:39,802 --> 00:08:43,522
And when something does go wrong,
the investigation has one purpose,

144
00:08:43,602 --> 00:08:45,642
written into international rules.

145
00:08:46,742 --> 00:08:48,402
To prevent the next accident.

146
00:08:49,082 --> 00:08:50,962
Not to decide who's to blame.

147
00:08:51,635 --> 00:08:53,795
That's the part that's easy to miss.

148
00:08:54,515 --> 00:08:55,995
People only tell you about their mistakes

149
00:08:56,225 --> 00:08:58,735
if they're not afraid
of what happens next.

150
00:09:01,395 --> 00:09:05,175
Safety experts like to picture this
as slices of Swiss cheese.

151
00:09:06,015 --> 00:09:08,575
The design, the checklist,
the crew, the training.

152
00:09:09,135 --> 00:09:11,255
Every slice has holes in it.

153
00:09:11,735 --> 00:09:15,435
An accident only happens
when the holes in every slice line up.

154
00:09:15,955 --> 00:09:20,395
And every crash, and every report,
adds a slice, or makes a hole smaller.

155
00:09:22,755 --> 00:09:25,275
But that system is run by people too.

156
00:09:25,595 --> 00:09:27,755
And it can be weakened from the inside.

157
00:09:29,256 --> 00:09:33,596
In October 2018,
a brand-new Boeing 737 MAX

158
00:09:33,826 --> 00:09:37,576
crashed into the sea off Indonesia,
minutes after takeoff.

159
00:09:38,355 --> 00:09:41,775
Less than five months later,
a second one crashed in Ethiopia.

160
00:09:42,005 --> 00:09:46,555
Three hundred and forty-six people died
in the two crashes.

161
00:09:47,727 --> 00:09:50,267
In both, new software
kept pushing the nose down,

162
00:09:50,497 --> 00:09:53,067
because of a single faulty sensor.

163
00:09:53,587 --> 00:09:58,367
Many of the pilots flying the MAX
hadn't been told the software was there.

164
00:09:58,897 --> 00:10:00,737
The evening before the first crash,

165
00:10:00,817 --> 00:10:04,117
another crew had faced the same problem
on the same aircraft.

166
00:10:04,617 --> 00:10:06,437
They switched the system off.

167
00:10:07,157 --> 00:10:09,377
The plane flew again the next morning.

168
00:10:10,837 --> 00:10:13,897
Investigators found failures
far from the cockpit.

169
00:10:14,657 --> 00:10:16,977
In how the aircraft was designed,

170
00:10:17,137 --> 00:10:19,617
how it was certified,
and how much of that checking

171
00:10:19,847 --> 00:10:22,277
the regulator had left
to the manufacturer.

172
00:10:23,074 --> 00:10:25,554
The slices of cheese were all still there.

173
00:10:26,034 --> 00:10:27,814
Some had just been made thinner.

174
00:10:29,734 --> 00:10:32,594
The MAX was grounded
for around twenty months.

175
00:10:32,674 --> 00:10:37,354
And the lesson went back into the system,
like every lesson before it.

176
00:10:39,624 --> 00:10:41,784
When several crashes make the headlines,

177
00:10:41,864 --> 00:10:44,804
it can feel as if flying
is getting more dangerous.

178
00:10:45,684 --> 00:10:48,484
But a single year is too short
to reveal a trend.

179
00:10:49,184 --> 00:10:51,804
Aviation safety is measured in decades.

180
00:10:54,624 --> 00:10:56,844
So why is flying so safe now?

181
00:10:57,244 --> 00:11:00,964
Not because planes stopped failing,
or pilots stopped making mistakes.

182
00:11:01,569 --> 00:11:03,829
But because every failure,
and every near miss,

183
00:11:04,059 --> 00:11:07,969
is taken apart, written down,
and shared with everyone who flies.

184
00:11:08,726 --> 00:11:10,606
None of it is there by accident.

185
00:11:11,286 --> 00:11:12,846
It's a collection of lessons.

186
00:11:12,926 --> 00:11:17,026
And some of them are written in the names
of people who never made it home.

187
00:11:18,856 --> 00:11:21,956
We didn't learn to fly safely
by never making mistakes.

188
00:11:22,616 --> 00:11:25,856
We learned by refusing
to make the same mistake twice.

189
00:11:29,176 --> 00:11:30,696
So here's a question for you.

190
00:11:30,776 --> 00:11:35,896
Aviation learned to treat mistakes
as information, not as something to hide.

191
00:11:36,676 --> 00:11:40,366
Where else do you think we need that most?
Let me know in the comments.
